    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 291847\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1147 AM PDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Chances for isolated to scattered storms linger, primarily in \n  northwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin.\n\n* Elsewhere, cooling temperatures and gusty southwest winds will be \n  the main story.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nLingering moisture and upper-level support from a Pacific trough \nwill spark isolated to scattered storms this afternoon. Best odds \n(20-50%) are in the southern Great Basin (where the best forcing is) \nand northwestern AZ (where the best moisture is). Relatively quick \nstorm motions and modest moisture should limit the flash flood \nthreat (going to need training storms). However, DCAPE values of \n1000-1500 J/kg and 20-35 knots of effective wind shear will keep \nthe threat of severe storms around. Tomorrow, ensemble guidance \nhas the tail end of the trough breaking off and digging into \ncentral CA. This pushes most of the moisture into our far \neastern/southeastern zones on Sunday, keeping PoPs confined to \nthese areas at 10-25%. However, models show this feature ejecting \non Monday with notable moisture advection ahead of it. Timing of \nthe shortwave ejection (relative to peak diurnal heating) is going\nto be critical for storm chances on Monday. Currently, most \nguidance brings it through during the morning and early afternoon,\nfavoring storms in eastern Mohave and Lincoln counties. A slower \ntrack could bring these better chances farther west. Beyond \nMonday, confidence in convective potential decreases due to \nuncertainty in Karina's (currently a tropical storm) interaction \nwith the broader Pacific trough. GEFS has the storm stronger and \nfarther northeast, allowing more moisture to be stripped off by \nthe trough and advected into the I-15 corridor and points south. \nThe ECMWF ensemble has the storm weaker and farther southwest, \nresulting in less moisture advection into our area. Ultimately, \nexpecting precipitation chances on Tuesday and beyond to be lower \n(10-20%), but cannot rule out a more active day during that time \nperiod if moisture advection is on the higher end.\n\nFor places that don't see precipitation (most of the Mojave Desert), \nthe main story is going to be the cooler temperatures and increased \nwinds. The presence of the aforementioned trough and mid-week high \nclouds streaming in off of Karina will cause temperatures to \nsteadily cool over the coming days. By Wednesday, forecast highs are \n~5 degrees below normal for early September. At the same time, the \nstronger southwest flow aloft will translate to gusty southwest \nwinds at the surface. Expecting widespread gusts of 20-35 mph \nthrough the forecast period, with isolated instances of 40-45 mph \npossible (40% chance) in places like Barstow, Kingman, and Desert \nRock. In the second half of the week, temperatures look to rebound \nback towards normal thanks to subtle height rises.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nsouth to southwest winds increase and become gusty after 19Z, with \noccasional gusts to 25 knots likely through sunset. Southwest winds \nwill diminish overnight, although occasional gusts to near 20 knots \nwill remain possible through Sunday morning. VFR conditions will \nprevail with cloud bases generally remaining AOA 12kft AGL. A few \nisolated thunderstorms are possible well east of the terminal area \nthis afternoon and evening, but some minor impacts to the eastern \napproaches may be encountered. Temperatures will exceed 100F through \n03Z this evening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Southerly winds will \nincrease across the region again this afternoon, with gusts to \naround 25 knots possible near most terminals. Winds will diminish \novernight. The exception will be across the western Mojave Desert, \nincluding KDAG, where winds will favor a westerly direction with \ngusts of 20 to 30 knots from mid-afternoon through late evening. \nIsolated areas of convection will develop across the southern Great \nBasin and western Arizona, with gusty erratic winds, lightning, and \nCIGs below 10kft possible with any storms that develop. Elsewhere, \nVFR conditions will prevail with cloud bases remaining AOA 12kft \nAGL.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
